WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.240
Hi, my name is Chris Gagne

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.020
and I'm an Agile coach.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.040
Today, I will talk about The Agile Manifesto.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.640
The Agile Manifesto was written in early 2001.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
A group of 17 very opinionated experts

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.240
specializing in extreme programming or "XP"

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.260
scrum, DSDM, and several other frameworks

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.200
got together for a weekend working session

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.200
at a beautiful ski Lodge in Utah.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.400
While they didn't agree on much,

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.560
they did come to agreement on this
 powerful Agile Manifesto.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:41.760
It describes the fundamental values upon which the principles and practices of Agile rest

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.960
if you are effectively delivering software in total harmony with these values and the principles

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.300
you are agile

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.800
So what's the manifesto? It's almost poetic. 
Goes like this:

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:04.620
We are uncovering better ways of developing software 
by doing it and helping others do it.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.799
Through this work, we have come to value

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.700
individuals and interactions 
over processes and tools

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.240
working software 
over comprehensive documentation

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.040
customer collaboration 
over contract negotiation

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.220
and responding to change 
over following a plan

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.400
That is while there's value in matters on the right, 
we value the items on the left more.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:40.580
Keep these values and the agile principles in mind 
as you inspect and adapt how your team works together

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:43.439
Good luck and have fun!

